= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Write $a=\alpha_2(T)$ and $u=\alpha_{2n}>0$. At high temperature $a>0$, so $f(m)=am^2+um^{2n}$ has one minimum at $m=0$. At low temperature $a<0$, the origin is a local maximum and two symmetry-related minima appear. The stationary equation is
$$
2m\{a+nu m^{2n-2}\}=0,
$$
so the <equilibrium magnetization> is
$$
m_{\rm eq}=0\quad(T\geq T_c),
$$
and
$$
m_{\rm eq}=\pm\left(\frac{-a}{nu}\right)^{1/(2n-2)}
\quad(T<T_c).
$$
Because $a(T)\sim T-T_c$, the two nonzero minima approach zero continuously as $T\uparrow T_c$. The <order parameter> is continuous while its response becomes singular, so this is a <continuous phase transition>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The ordered solution gives
$$
|m_{\rm eq}|\sim(T_c-T)^{1/(2n-2)},
$$
so the <order-parameter critical exponent> is
$$
\boxed{\beta=\frac1{2(n-1)}}.
$$
At $T=T_c$, adding a magnetic term $-hm$ gives $h=2nu m^{2n-1}$, hence
$$
\boxed{\delta=2n-1}.
$$
Above $T_c$, the zero-field susceptibility is $\chi=(2a)^{-1}$. Below $T_c$, the curvature at a minimum is $-4(n-1)a$, so both sides give
$$
\boxed{\gamma=1}.
$$
At the ordered minimum, $a=-nu m^{2n-2}$ and
$$
f_{\rm sing}=(1-n)u m^{2n}\sim-|T-T_c|^{n/(n-1)}.
$$
Since $f_{\rm sing}\sim|T-T_c|^{2-\alpha}$, the <heat-capacity critical exponent> is
$$
\boxed{\alpha=\frac{n-2}{n-1}}.
$$
These values obey the <Widom scaling relation> and <Rushbrooke scaling relation> at mean-field level.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <Landau-Ginzburg theory> promotes the order parameter to a field and penalizes spatial gradients:
$$
F[m]=\int d^dx\left[
\frac\kappa2|\nabla m|^2+\alpha_2(T)m^2
+\alpha_{2n}m^{2n}-hm
\right],
$$
with stiffness $\kappa>0$. Further even powers and higher-derivative terms may be included when required by accuracy.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Mean field gives
$$
C_{\rm MF}\sim|T-T_c|^{-\alpha}
=|T-T_c|^{-(n-2)/(n-1)}.
$$
The stated Gaussian fluctuation term is
$$
C_{\rm fl}\sim|T-T_c|^{d/2-2}.
$$
As $T\to T_c$, mean field is more singular precisely when
$$
-\frac{n-2}{n-1}<\frac d2-2,
$$
or
$$
d>\frac{2n}{n-1}.
$$
Thus the <upper critical dimension> for the $m^{2n}$ interaction is
$$
\boxed{d_c=\frac{2n}{n-1}}.
$$

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $z=2d$ be the coordination number of the hypercubic lattice. The mean interaction energy per site is $(Jz/2)m_em_o=Jd,m_em_o$. Define
$$
I(m)=\frac{1+m}{2}\log\frac{1+m}{2}
+\frac{1-m}{2}\log\frac{1-m}{2}.
$$
The two sublattices each contain half the sites, so the mean-field free energy per site is
$$
\boxed{
f(\beta,m_e,m_o)
=Jd\,m_em_o-\frac{h+g}{2}m_e-\frac{h-g}{2}m_o
+\frac1{2\beta}\{I(m_e)+I(m_o)\}.}
$$
The entropy terms are the binary-spin mixing entropies and the positive $J$ favors opposite sublattice magnetizations.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Since $I'(m)=\operatorname{artanh}m$, stationarity gives
$$
\operatorname{artanh}m_e
=\beta(h+g-2Jd\,m_o),
$$
$$
\operatorname{artanh}m_o
=\beta(h-g-2Jd\,m_e).
$$
Therefore
$$
m_e=\tanh[\beta A(m_o)],
\qquad
m_o=\tanh[\beta D(m_e)],
$$
where
$$
\boxed{A(m_o)=h+g-2Jd\,m_o,
\qquad D(m_e)=h-g-2Jd\,m_e.}
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For $h=g=0$, the expansion $I(m)=-\log2+m^2/2+O(m^4)$ gives
$$
f={\rm constant}+Jd\,m_em_o
+\frac1{4\beta}(m_e^2+m_o^2)+O(m^4).
$$
With $m_\pm=(m_e\pm m_o)/2$, one has $m_em_o=m_+^2-m_-^2$ and $m_e^2+m_o^2=2(m_+^2+m_-^2)$. Hence
$$
f={\rm constant}
+\left(Jd+\frac1{2\beta}\right)m_+^2
+\left(\frac1{2\beta}-Jd\right)m_-^2+O(m^4).
$$
The <staggered magnetization> $m_-$ becomes unstable at
$$
\boxed{T_c=2Jd},
$$
producing the antiferromagnetic transition. The coefficient of the uniform magnetization $m_+$ remains positive for every $T>0$, so there is no $m_+$ transition.

= 2
{parent=Paper 303}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A momentum-shell <renormalization group> step has three parts:

1. Split $\phi=\phi_<+\phi_>$ into slow modes $|k|<\Lambda/\zeta$ and fast modes $\Lambda/\zeta<|k|<\Lambda$, then integrate out $\phi_>$ using a <cumulant expansion> to obtain a <Wilsonian effective action> for $\phi_<$.
2. Rescale momenta by $k'=\zeta k$, equivalently coordinates by $x'=x/\zeta$, restoring the cutoff from $\Lambda/\zeta$ to $\Lambda$.
3. Rescale the field by $\phi'(x')=\zeta^{(d-2)/2}\phi_<(x)$ at the <Gaussian fixed point>, restoring the normalized kinetic term.

The resulting functional has the original form and cutoff but new masses and couplings. Iteration produces a <renormalization-group flow>.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Requiring $\int d^dx\,(\nabla\phi)^2$ to be dimensionless gives the <engineering dimensions>
$$
[\phi]=\frac{d-2}{2},
\qquad [\mu_0]=1,
$$
and
$$
[\lambda_0]=d-3[\phi]=\frac{6-d}{2},
$$
$$
[g_0]=d-4[\phi]=4-d,
$$
$$
[\gamma_0]=d-6[\phi]=6-2d.
$$
**Thus $\mu_0$ is relevant. The cubic coupling is relevant, marginal, or irrelevant for $d<6$, $d=6$, or $d>6$; the quartic coupling has the same classification around $d=4$; and the sextic coupling has it around $d=3$.**

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let
$$
G_>(x-y)=\int_{\Lambda/\zeta<|q|<\Lambda}
\frac{d^dq}{(2\pi)^d}\frac{e^{iq\cdot(x-y)}}{q^2+\mu_0^2}.
$$
At order $g_0^2$, the connected six-point topology consists of two quartic vertices joined by one fast line. The second cumulant gives
$$
-8g_0^2\int d^dx\,d^dy\,
\phi_<^3(x)G_>(x-y)\phi_<^3(y).
$$
For a sharp shell and external slow momenta approaching zero, the connecting momentum cannot lie in the fast shell, so this nonlocal tree topology gives no local zero-momentum $\phi_<^6$ coupling.

At order $g_0^3$, the local connected contribution is the triangle of three quartic vertices, with two external slow legs at each vertex and one fast propagator along each edge. The factor is
$$
\frac1{3!}\binom42^3(2^2)=144.
$$
Consequently, at zero external momentum,
$$
\Delta\gamma
=144g_0^3I_3(\zeta),
\qquad
I_3(\zeta)=\int_{\Lambda/\zeta<|q|<\Lambda}
\frac{d^dq}{(2\pi)^d}\frac1{(q^2+\mu_0^2)^3}.
$$
Ignoring contributions involving $\lambda_0,\gamma_0$ and anomalous field rescaling, the running sextic coupling is therefore
$$
\boxed{
\gamma(\zeta)=\zeta^{6-2d}
\left[\gamma_0+144g_0^3I_3(\zeta)\right]+O(g_0^4).}
$$
The diagrams are respectively the one-line two-vertex six-point tree and the three-vertex triangular one-loop six-point diagram; only the latter contributes to the local shell coupling at vanishing external momenta.

